<style>
  /* Pin the preview to the device's own profile.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     TRMNL's framework carries a per-device block — `.screen--ogv2`,
     `.screen--og`, `.screen--amazon_kindle_2024` — setting that
     hardware's screen size, pixel ratio, gap scale and colour depth
     on `.screen`. TRMNL stamps the right class on the wrapper when it
     renders for a device.

     `trmnlp` does not. It emits a bare `class="screen
     screen--fonts-trmnl"`, so a local build falls back to the `:root`
     defaults and renders a board no device will ever show. A `<style>`
     here cannot add a class to that wrapper, but it can set the same
     variables the class would — before trmnlp takes its screenshot,
     so the PNG gets them too.

     Values copied verbatim from `.trmnl .screen--ogv2` in
     https://trmnl.com/css/3.4.4/plugins.css. `tests/layouts.test.ts`
     checks they still match, because a framework upgrade that retunes
     a device would otherwise drift silently.

     For this device at scale Regular every value here already matches
     what the fallback computes, so this block changes nothing today.
     It is pinned so that a framework upgrade which retunes the device
     surfaces as a test failure rather than as a surprise on the panel.

     **--quadrant-w/h are deliberately not copied.** The device sets
     them to 100%, but that resolves against trmnlp's own unsized
     wrapper and collapses the quadrant view to an empty title bar —
     verified by rendering it. They are the one pair whose device value
     depends on markup trmnlp does not reproduce, so the fallback
     arithmetic is what keeps the local quadrant readable.

     The *other* half of matching the device is --modifier-scale, which
     is a per-plugin user setting (screen--scale-small .. xxlarge) and
     not a device property. It is left alone deliberately: the default
     of 1 is scale Regular. If the panel is set to anything else, the
     preview will not match and this block is not the place to fix it. */
  .trmnl .screen {
    --screen-w: 800px;
    --screen-h: 480px;
    --screen-w-original: 800px;
    --screen-h-original: 480px;
    --pixel-ratio: 1;
    --dither-pixel-ratio: 1;
    --device-ui-scale: 1;
    --gap-scale: 1;
    --color-depth: 2;
    --density-tier: 1x;
    --device-name: "ogv2";
  }
</style>

<style>
  /* MTA route bullets: a circle for local service, a diamond for express.
     The feed names an express variant by suffixing X - 6X and 7X are the
     ones that run today - so the X is a shape, not part of the route name,
     and is stripped before display.

     Framework labels are pills, and it has no circle or diamond utility, so
     the two shapes are defined here. Sizes are in em so they follow whatever
     element size the label is given at each breakpoint; this block sets no
     font-size of its own. */
  .bullet {
    /* 1.7em overflowed a heading once a complex carried four routes, and
       stacked a row's text below its own bullet instead of beside it. The
       framework's type does not step down at this device size — every label
       size resolves to the same 16px — so width has to come from the glyph
       box rather than from a smaller face. */
    width: 1.35em;
    height: 1.35em;
    /* The framework's own .label padding lands later in the cascade and
       stretches the box back into a rounded rectangle once the glyph is
       two characters wide. Pinning min/max as well as width is what keeps
       SI a circle at the smaller layouts' label sizes. */
    min-width: 1.35em;
    max-width: 1.35em;
    padding: 0;
    border-radius: 50%;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Centres the glyph on the row's text baseline box. Without it a bullet
       sits optically high beside the destination it labels. */
    align-items: center;
    line-height: 1;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* The glyph, not the circle.
     `.bullet` sizes itself in `em`, so putting the reduction on the bullet
     would shrink the circle with it and break the shared centre line the
     column is read down. The span inside carries it instead: the numeral
     sits a touch smaller in a circle that has not moved. */
  .bullet > span {
    font-size: 0.92em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .bullet--express {
    border-radius: 0;
    /* A rotated square's bounding box grows by root 2, so a diamond would sit
       29px wide beside a 20px circle and push its row out of line. Scaling by
       1/root 2 keeps the two occupying the same space. */
    transform: rotate(45deg) scale(0.707);
  }
  /* Counter-rotate the glyph so the number stays upright in the diamond. */
  .bullet--express > span {
    display: block;
    /* Undo both, so the glyph stays upright and full size in the diamond. */
    transform: rotate(-45deg) scale(1.414);
  }
  /* Two letters in a circle sized for one would touch the curve on both
     sides, so SI - the only two-character bullet in the system - is set
     smaller and tighter, which is what the MTA's own SI bullet does. The
     circle keeps its diameter, so it still lines up with every other
     bullet in the column. */
  .bullet--pair > span {
    /* 0.78em cleared the curve but rendered SI as a smudge in the smaller
       row labels on a 1-bit panel. 0.88em with the tracking pulled in is
       the most type that still fits, and stays legible at row size. */
    font-size: 0.88em;
    letter-spacing: -0.06em;
    /* The circle's padding is zero, so without this the tracking on the
       final letter pushes SI visibly left of centre. */
    text-indent: -0.06em;
  }
  /* The bullet strip takes what the station name leaves and clips there.

     It was a fixed count — four in `full`, three in the smaller layouts —
     set when bullets were 1.7em and a twelve-route complex wrapped onto a
     second line. Any fixed count is the wrong shape: Times Square has
     twelve routes, so a cap silently drops routes the rows below still
     show, and the heading ends up contradicting its own card. Clipping
     instead means what fits is shown and the cap cannot go stale against a
     future type size.

     `flex-shrink` lets the name win when both cannot fit, and `nowrap`
     keeps the heading one line tall. */
  /* The name is what identifies a card, so it is never crushed below a
     readable minimum — twelve bullets at Times Square took the heading
     down to "Times" before this. The strip yields first and clips. */
  .card-name {
    min-width: 7ch;
  }
  .bullet-strip {
    display: flex;
    /* A ceiling as well as shrink, so a long route list cannot claim the
       heading. Half is enough for six marks, which is as many as tell a
       rider anything the name has not. */
    max-width: 50%;
    /* Not `inherit`: that takes the heading row's `gap--small` (7px), which
       is the right distance between the station name and the strip but too
       much between the bullets themselves — a route list reads as one
       object, and 7px at a 1.35em bullet spaces it out into separate marks.
       4px groups them while keeping the circles clear of each other. */
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 0;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
  }
  /* A heading tag names the network — LIRR, Metro North, Bus, Ferry — and
     shares its slot with the subway's route bullets. Unlike a bullet it is
     words, so it needs bounding: without this a long one wraps onto a second
     line, pushes past the card's edge into the heading beside it, and
     crushes the station name it was meant to sit next to.

     The station name is what a rider reads first, so the tag is the one that
     gives way: it will not grow past a third of the heading, will not wrap,
     and ellipsizes when it must. */
  .type-tag {
    /* Smaller than the heading it sits beside, so a four-bullet complex and
       a long network name both clear the card edge. */
    font-size: 0.82em;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    max-width: 33%;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
    display: block;
  }
  /* Rows are one line each, so the space two lines used to take is given
     back as breathing room between them — the panel is read at a glance
     from across a room, and rows packed tight are what make it hard to
     track one across its columns. */
  .row-line {
    padding-top: 0.14em;
    padding-bottom: 0.14em;
  }
  /* The clock is fixed width and never gives way. Without this the flex row
     squeezes it like any other text child, and a long destination pushes
     the digits into the badge beside them — the destination is what should
     yield, since a half-read station name is still useful and a half-read
     departure time is not. */
  .row-time {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    /* Separates the digits from the destination clipping into them. */
    padding-left: 0.2em;
  }
  /* A subway card splits into two direction columns, so its rows get half
     the width every other card's do — 122px against 253px. Badge, clock and
     gaps take 78px of that, leaving 44px for the destination: six
     characters, which renders "Coney Island-Stillwell Av" as "Cone…".

     The clock was dropped a step to buy the name back, and that went too
     far — at 0.75em the split cards' times read visibly smaller than the
     full-width ones beside them, and the time is the one thing on the row
     that must stay legible from across a room. A destination clipped to
     eight characters is accepted instead; the column heading already says
     which way the train is going.

     0.92em is a hair under the row's size, enough to keep two columns of
     digits from colliding without reading as a different size. */
  .row-line--split .row-time {
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
  /* The destination takes whatever the badge and the clock leave, and clips
     rather than wrapping: a wrapped name would make its row taller than its
     neighbours, and on a fixed panel a name cut mid-word still reads. */
  .row-dest {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: clip;
  }
  /* A row badge is a route name — a railroad branch, a bus route — and some
     are long: "City Terminal Zone" is LIRR's name for Penn/Atlantic/Grand
     Central service. Unbounded it wraps to a second line and makes that one
     row taller than its neighbours, which is what uniform row height exists
     to prevent: a column you can scan down.

     So a badge never wraps. It keeps its own width where it fits and
     ellipsizes where it does not, and the destination beside it gives way
     first — the route is what a rider scans for. */
  .row-badge {
    flex-shrink: 1;
    /* A floor in `ch`, not `em` or a percentage. The framework's `.label`
       sets its own `max-width: 100%` and `.label--small` reduces the font
       size, so an `em` floor shrinks with the type it is meant to protect
       and a percentage is measured against a box the flex row has already
       squeezed. `ch` is the width of a "0" in the rendered face, so 4.5ch
       is four characters plus the label's padding whatever size it lands
       at — enough that "M42" never becomes "M…", which reads as a
       different route rather than as a truncation. */
    min-width: 4.5ch;
    /* Longer railroad branches still ellipsize; the first word separates
       "Port Washington" from "Port Jefferson". */
    max-width: 9ch;
    /* A railroad or bus badge sits in the same column as a subway bullet
       and must read as the same kind of mark. The framework's `.label`
       padding made it visibly taller — "Far Rock…" stood a third higher
       than the ⑦ beside it — so the box is pinned to the bullet's own
       1.35em and the type stepped down to fit inside it. */
    height: 1.35em;
    font-size: 0.86em;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
</style>

{% comment %}
  The board: three columns, each holding one or two cards.

  Slots map to the grid in fixed positions — 1/2 left, 3/4 middle, 5/6 right —
  so a station stays where the user put it. A column with one active slot gives
  that card the full height; with two, they split it with a rule between.

  Rows are trimmed by the framework's Content Limiter rather than by the
  backend: `data-content-limiter` on a `.content` element measures the rendered
  height in the browser and drops what does not fit. The backend therefore
  sends more rows than any card can show (16) and the panel decides, so a card
  alone in its column fills the space without the server knowing the layout.
  The explicit `data-content-max-height` is what makes the cut land between
  rows instead of through one.

  Row shape, matching the dashboard it replaces:
    [badge] [destination, clipped] [time, right-aligned]

  Rows are data tuples, not objects, to stay inside TRMNL's 5KB payload cap:
    r[0] badge   r[1] destination   r[2] time   r[3] 1 when urgent
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Heights the Content Limiter trims to, measured against the rendered panel.

  Scaled by device: a TRMNL X is 1404px tall against the OG's 480, so a budget
  tuned for one leaves most of the other empty. `trmnl.device.height` is what
  the platform reports, so the budget follows the hardware rather than being
  guessed per model.

  The ratios come from the OG measurement — 374 of 480 usable for a full card,
  163 for one sharing a column — applied to whatever height the device has.
  A percentage point either way decides whether the last row lands whole or
  leaves a fragment, so these were tuned against the render rather than
  reasoned about.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign device_height = trmnl.device.height | default: 480 %}
{% assign full_height = device_height | times: 72 | divided_by: 100 %}
{% assign split_height = device_height | times: 34 | divided_by: 100 %}

{% comment %}
  Two things the first renders exposed.

  **A badge must not wrap.** A railroad branch name runs onto a second line
  otherwise, doubling the row height and pushing the card over its budget. A
  one-line clamp keeps every row identical.

  **A badge must not be squeezed either.** The clamp alone let a long
  destination crush the badge — the bus card rendered a four-character "Q101"
  as "Q1…" while fifteen-character railroad badges beside it were fine, because
  "STEINWAY via STEINWAY ST" was taking the width.

  The fix is simply to leave the badge alone: no clamp, no width. `data-clamp`
  was what broke it — the clamp engine measures a badge the flex row has
  already squeezed and truncates to fit that squeezed box, so a long
  destination two elements away decided how much of "Q101" survived.

  Without it the badge sizes to its own text and the destination gives way
  instead, which is the right priority: the route is what a rider scans for.
  A fixed width was also tried and is wrong in both directions — far too wide
  for a one-character subway bullet, too narrow for "Port Washington".

  **A row is one line: badge, destination, time.** It was two for a while,
  on the measurement that a split subway column (~123px) left only 43px for
  the destination once a 1.7em bullet, the clock and the gaps had taken
  theirs — six characters, against names like "Coney Island-Stillwell Av".

  The bullet is 1.35em now, which returns most of that width, and one line
  per row buys back the vertical space the second line was spending. The
  destination clips rather than wrapping: a wrapped name would make its row
  taller than its neighbours, and on a fixed panel a name cut mid-word still
  reads.

  **The time is set at `title--small`, not `value--*`.** The smallest value
  size is 20px and took about 55px of a 90px direction column — well over half
  the width for a clock. At 16px it matches the destination beside it and
  leaves room for the destination to be shown at all.

  **`.content` caps itself at a prose-reading width.** The framework sets
  `max-width: var(--richtext-content-max-width)` on it, which is right for
  paragraphs and wrong for a departure column: it left a full-width card using
  169px of its 253, and squeezed the subway columns to 87px. `w--full`
  overrides it.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Split a route id into the character shown and the shape that shows it.
  "7X" is the 7 running express, drawn as a diamond; "7" is a circle.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  How many of the three columns hold a card.

  The grid is sized to that rather than fixed at three, so a board using two
  columns fills the panel instead of leaving a third of it blank. Two columns
  go from ~253px each to ~390px — and width is the thing destinations have
  been short of all along, which is why three is the maximum in the first
  place.

  Slots keep their columns: 1/2 left, 3/4 middle, 5/6 right. Only the empty
  columns are dropped, so a card never moves because a different card was
  added elsewhere.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign used_columns = 0 %}
{% for column_index in (0..2) %}
  {% assign first = column_index | times: 2 | plus: 1 %}
  {% assign second = first | plus: 1 %}
  {% for card in c %}
    {% if card.s == first or card.s == second %}
      {% assign used_columns = used_columns | plus: 1 %}
      {% break %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% if used_columns == 0 %}{% assign used_columns = 1 %}{% endif %}

<div class="view">
  <div class="layout">
    <div class="grid grid--cols-{{ used_columns }} h--full">

      {% comment %}
        Left column takes slots 1 and 2, middle 3 and 4, right 5 and 6.

        Compared as numbers rather than by splitting a string: the backend
        sends `s` as a number, and a split string yields strings, so `1 == "1"`
        is false in Liquid and the whole board renders blank.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% for column_index in (0..2) %}
        {% assign top_slot = column_index | times: 2 | plus: 1 %}
        {% assign bottom_slot = top_slot | plus: 1 %}

        {% comment %}
          How many of this column's two slots the user configured. A slot left
          Off never reaches the board, so a column may hold two cards, one, or
          none — and a lone card takes the column's whole height.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% assign card_count = 0 %}
        {% for card in c %}
          {% if card.s == top_slot or card.s == bottom_slot %}
            {% assign card_count = card_count | plus: 1 %}
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% comment %}
          An empty column is skipped entirely rather than rendered blank —
          otherwise the grid would reserve a track for it and the reflow
          above would buy nothing.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% if card_count > 0 %}
        <div class="col gap--small h--full{% if card_count < 2 %} col--start{% endif %}">
          {% for card in c %}
            {% if card.s == top_slot or card.s == bottom_slot %}
              {% comment %}
                Each card in a split column claims half the height whatever it
                holds. Without this the divider lands wherever the top card
                happens to end — a two-row bus card put it a quarter of the
                way down and gave the card below three quarters of the column,
                so two boards with the same layout looked different.

                The column drops `col--start` when it is split: that sets
                `justify-content: flex-start`, which packs children to the top
                and overrides the even share. A single-card column keeps it,
                so a lone card still sits at the top rather than centring.
              {% endcomment %}
              <div class="{% if card_count > 1 %}grow basis--0{% endif %} w--full">

                {% comment %} A rule between two cards sharing a column. {% endcomment %}
                {% if card.s == bottom_slot and card_count > 1 %}
                  <div class="border--h-black w--full"></div>
                {% endif %}

                {% comment %}
                  Card heading. Subway cards carry route bullets; the others
                  are named by their station alone.
                {% endcomment %}
                {% comment %}
                  Station name first, bullets after.

                  Leading with the bullets crushed "Times Sq-42 St" to "Ti..."
                  — the name is what identifies a card, so it takes the space.

                  The bullets were capped at four, from when they were 1.7em
                  and a twelve-route complex wrapped onto a second line,
                  making one heading taller than the rest. At 1.35em six fit,
                  but any fixed cap is the wrong shape: Times Square has
                  twelve, so a cap silently drops routes the rows below are
                  still showing, and the heading contradicts its own card.

                  So the strip takes the width the name leaves and clips
                  there. What fits is shown, nothing is dropped at a number
                  that no longer matches the type size, and the row stays one
                  line tall because the strip cannot wrap.
                {% endcomment %}
                <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--small">
                  <span class="card-name title title--small md:title--base lg:title--large grow" data-clamp="1">{{ card.n }}</span>
                  {% if card.b %}
                    <div class="bullet-strip">
                    {% for route in card.b %}
                      {% assign is_express = false %}
                      {% assign glyph = route %}
                      {% if route.size > 1 and route contains "X" %}
                        {% assign stripped = route | remove_last: "X" %}
                        {% if stripped.size == 1 %}
                          {% assign is_express = true %}
                          {% assign glyph = stripped %}
                        {% endif %}
                      {% endif %}
                      {% comment %}
                    A card showing two direction columns gives each half the
                    width, which is what the tighter row variant is for.
                  {% endcomment %}
                  {% assign split_class = "" %}
                  {% if card.k.size > 1 %}
                    {% assign split_class = " row-line--split" %}
                  {% endif %}
                  {% comment %}
                        A bullet holds one or two characters. One covers every
                        subway route and the shuttles; two covers SI, the
                        Staten Island Railway, which the R211 trains now
                        running the line sign as a circular SI bullet.

                        Subway cards only: the shape means "a subway line",
                        so it is scoped by card type rather than by length.
                        Three or more characters is a bus route or a railroad
                        branch, which stays a pill — "Babylon" in a circle is
                        unreadable.
                      {% endcomment %}
                      {% if card.y == "subway" and glyph.size <= 2 %}
                        <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled bullet{% if glyph.size == 2 %} bullet--pair{% endif %}{% if is_express %} bullet--express{% endif %}"><span>{{ glyph }}</span></span>
                      {% else %}
                        <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled type-tag">{{ glyph }}</span>
                      {% endif %}
                    {% endfor %}
                  </div>
                  
                  {% endif %}
                </div>
                <div class="border--h-40 w--full"></div>

                {% if card.e %}
                  {% comment %}
                    A failed fetch, reported in place. Distinct from a card
                    with nothing due — that is a fact about the service, not a
                    fault, and says so below instead.
                  {% endcomment %}
                  <span class="label label--small label--outline">Unavailable</span>
                  <span class="description" data-clamp="2">{{ card.e }}</span>

                {% else %}
                  {% if card_count == 1 %}
                    {% assign card_height = full_height %}
                  {% else %}
                    {% assign card_height = split_height %}
                  {% endif %}

                  {% comment %}
                    A card's columns sit **side by side**, not stacked.

                    A subway card has two — one per direction — and stacking
                    them put the second inside the same height budget as the
                    first, so the limiter cut it off entirely and the card
                    looked like a terminal that only ran one way. Bus cards
                    have one column per stop ID, which is one direction each,
                    so the same rule applies to them.

                    Each gets `grow basis--0` so they divide the card evenly
                    whatever they hold, and its own limiter so a busy
                    direction cannot crowd out a quiet one. The row carries
                    `flex--top`, without which the shorter column centres
                    itself vertically and the two headings no longer line up.
                  {% endcomment %}

                  <div class="flex flex--row gap--small w--full flex--top">
                    {% comment %}
                      When every departure in a column goes to the same place,
                      the destination repeats down the column and tells the
                      rider nothing — every northbound 7 at Vernon Blvd ends
                      at Flushing-Main St. Dropping it matters here because
                      two side-by-side columns leave about 40px for a
                      destination, which is unreadable, while the badge and
                      time are what a rider actually needs.
                    {% endcomment %}
                    {% for col in card.k %}
                    <div class="grow basis--0 content w--full"
                         data-content-limiter="true"
                         data-content-max-height="{{ card_height }}">
                      {% if col.h != "" %}
                        <div class="flex flex--row w--full">
                          <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--underline">{{ col.h }}</span>
                        </div>
                      {% endif %}

                      {% for r in col.r %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Every row keeps its own destination.

                          An earlier version hoisted it into the column
                          heading when a column's departures all shared one,
                          which is wrong for this network: subway lines
                          branch, and a stop usually carries several routes.
                          Southbound at Jay St-MetroTech is A, C and F with
                          five distinct terminals — Euclid Av, Lefferts Blvd,
                          Church Av, Far Rockaway, Coney Island — and naming
                          one of them above the column would tell a rider to
                          board the wrong train. Coney Island-Stillwell is a
                          single stop ID carrying D, F, N and Q.

                          The only destination worth suppressing is one that
                          repeats its own badge, which is a railroad habit:
                          a branch's trains usually end at the branch's
                          namesake, so "Hempstead | Hempstead" says it twice.
                          A destination that merely extends the badge is
                          shortened to the part that differs — "New Haven"
                          beside "New Haven-State St" becomes "State St" —
                          which also stops the badge wrapping onto two lines.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% comment %}
                          A subway route is one character — or two, for SI —
                          so it gets a bullet: circle for local, diamond for
                          express, with the feed's X suffix stripped since the
                          shape carries it.

                          **Subway cards only.** The shape itself means "a
                          subway line", so it has to be scoped by card type
                          and not by glyph length. Ferry routes are two
                          characters — AS, ER, SG — and were being drawn as
                          subway bullets; bus routes escaped only by being
                          four characters long. A named route stays a pill:
                          "Babylon" in a circle is unreadable.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% assign is_express = false %}
                        {% assign glyph = r[0] %}
                        {% if r[0].size > 1 and r[0] contains "X" %}
                          {% assign stripped = r[0] | remove_last: "X" %}
                          {% if stripped.size == 1 %}
                            {% assign is_express = true %}
                            {% assign glyph = stripped %}
                          {% endif %}
                        {% endif %}
                        {% if card.y == "subway" and glyph.size <= 2 %}
                          {% assign bullet_class = "bullet" %}
                          {% if glyph.size == 2 %}
                            {% assign bullet_class = "bullet bullet--pair" %}
                          {% endif %}
                          {% if is_express %}
                            {% assign bullet_class = "bullet bullet--express" %}
                          {% endif %}
                        {% else %}
                          {% assign bullet_class = "" %}
                        {% endif %}
                        {% assign destination = r[1] %}
                        {% if destination == r[0] %}
                          {% assign destination = "" %}
                        {% else %}
                          {% assign badge_prefix = r[0] | append: "-" %}
                          {% if destination contains badge_prefix %}
                            {% assign destination = destination | remove_first: badge_prefix %}
                          {% endif %}
                        {% endif %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Urgent departures — the ones you must leave for now
                          — are filled; everything else is outlined.

                          This was the other way round, on the reasoning that
                          at a ten-minute threshold most rows qualify and a
                          panel of solid black marks has no emphasis left.
                          The emphasis is the wrong way to read it: solid
                          black is what the eye lands on first, so it should
                          mark the departure that needs acting on rather than
                          the ones that do not. If most rows end up filled,
                          the threshold is too generous — that is a setting,
                          not something to compensate for in the styling.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% if r[3] == 1 %}
                          {% assign badge_fill = "label--filled" %}
                        {% else %}
                          {% assign badge_fill = "label--outline" %}
                        {% endif %}

                        {% comment %}
                          `‹` toward the city, `›` away from it, nothing when
                          the direction is unknown — a train terminating here
                          has none, and no other transit type reports one.
                          Single-angle quotes rather than ← →, which the
                          panel's face draws inconsistently at this size.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        {% assign arrow = "" %}
                        {% if r[4] == 1 %}
                          {% assign arrow = "‹" %}
                        {% elsif r[4] == -1 %}
                          {% assign arrow = "›" %}
                        {% endif %}

                        {% comment %}
                          Every row is one line: badge, destination, time.

                          A split subway column was two lines until now, on
                          the measurement that ~123px cannot carry all three
                          — and it could not, at a 1.7em bullet. The bullet
                          is 1.35em now, which returns the width the
                          destination needed, and one line per row buys back
                          the vertical space two lines were spending.

                          The destination clips rather than wrapping. A name
                          cut mid-word still reads on a fixed panel, and a
                          wrapped one would make its row taller than its
                          neighbours — which is what uniform row height
                          exists to prevent.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        <div class="row-line{{ split_class }} flex flex--row flex--between flex--center-y gap--small w--full">
                          <span class="label label--small md:label--base {{ badge_fill }} {{ bullet_class }}{% if bullet_class == "" %} row-badge{% endif %}">{% if bullet_class != "" %}<span>{{ glyph }}</span>{% else %}{{ r[0] }}{% endif %}</span>
                          {% comment %}
                            The arrow stands on its own when the destination
                            was dropped as redundant — a train to its
                            branch's namesake still has a direction, and
                            losing it there would make the rows that most
                            need it the ones without it.
                          {% endcomment %}
                          <span class="row-dest description grow" data-clamp="1">{% if arrow != "" %}{{ arrow }}&nbsp;{% endif %}{{ destination }}</span>
                          <span class="row-time title title--small md:title--base">{{ r[2] }}</span>
                        </div>
                      {% endfor %}

                      {% assign row_count = col.r | size %}
                      {% if row_count == 0 %}
                        {% comment %}
                          Empty is not an error: the feed answered and had
                          nothing due. Saying so is the whole point — the app
                          does not guess whether service ended, the stop is
                          peak-only, or everything is inside the walk time.
                        {% endcomment %}
                        <span class="description">No upcoming departures</span>
                      {% endif %}
                    </div>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </div>
                {% endif %}
              </div>
            {% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}

      {% endfor %}

    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="title_bar">
    {% comment %}
        The data's age, in New York time. A panel refreshes on an interval
        and an e-ink screen holds its last image through a failure, so
        without this there is no way to tell a quiet board from a frozen
        one. Formatted by the Worker, because Liquid cannot convert a
        timezone and the panel runs wherever the user is.
      {% endcomment %}
      <span class="title">NYC Transit{% if a %} · as of {{ a }}{% endif %}</span>
    {% if d %}
      {% comment %}
        The backend substitutes a demo board when nothing is configured, so
        the panel must say whose stations these are. Without it a new
        installer sees Times Square and reasonably assumes their own form
        took effect.
      {% endcomment %}
      <span class="instance">Example - choose your stations in settings</span>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</div>
